draft_text = """=== LEAF p51 ===
There is given a briefer description of the Eucharist than previously:
**"And they who are well to do, and willing, give what each thinks fit; and what is collected is deposited with the president, and he takes care of orphans and widows... And on the day called Sunday [day of the Sun] all who live in cities or in the country gather together to one place, for it is the first day on which God, having wrought a change in the darkness and matter, made the world; and Jesus Christ our Savior on the same day rose from the dead"** [^283].

St. Justin says nothing of the time of day for this liturgical assembly, but the fact that this assembly was preceded by baptism does not allow us to assign it unto the morning and consequently to identify it with the pre-dawn assembly of Pliny, as older church historians did, naively trusting Pliny's bureaucratically boastful assertion that he had compelled Christians to discontinue their evening repasts, and supposing that from Trajan onward they were forced to transfer the Eucharist unto the morning. Thus the practice described in Justin, presumably, differs in nothing from that of Pliny.

Yet Justin's testimony is important because it already indicates a fully established ritual of the Eucharist, which has a preparatory part rather noticeably separated from it, consisting of reading and prayer. This is the part that subsequently formed what came to be called *ta typika* of the Liturgy (or the Liturgy of the Catechumens) in distinction from its canon [anaphora], and what served as the prototype of Vespers. Furthermore, in Justin the partaking of the Eucharist is clearly posited before the charitable repast (the agape). Of such a practice speaks also the apocryphal *Acts of John*, according to which the Apostle with a certain Andronicus performs a commemoration upon the tomb of the latter's wife on the third day after her death: after the Eucharist, for which the deceased woman also arose, the faithful partook of food [^284]. At the same time, St. Justin ascertains the transition of the agapes into a purely charitable institution [^285]. In another place St. Justin supplements the information communicated here concerning the evening divine service, noting that at it **"solemn rites and hymns (*pompas kai *(Orig. p. 55)* hymnous*) are offered through the word for our creation, for all means unto our well-being, for various kinds of produce, for the changes of the seasons, and petitions are sent up that we may rise again in incorruption"** [^286]. (The content of these prayers is close unto the prayers of the *Teaching of the Twelve Apostles*.) Remarkable here is the mention of solemn rites during divine worship, something new in comparison with other monuments (perhaps lity processions, entrances for prayers).

### The Agape according to Lucian

---

=== LEAF p52 ===
The satirist Lucian of Samosata (second half of the 2nd cent.) attributes unto the founder of Christianity the institution of "new mysteries"—calling Christian worship evidently by this term—and recounts that when Peregrinus, the hero of the satire, who had by deceit achieved among Christians the most prominent position, having become among them a prophet, *thiasarchēs*, *synagōgeus*, and president, was cast into prison, "from the very earliest dawn there stood at the prison gates a crowd of old women, widows, and orphans." "And among them the leaders of the sect, bribing the guards, spent the entire night with him; partaking of elaborate suppers (*deipna poikila*), they read their sacred books... By virtue of his imprisonment, large sums of money were brought unto Peregrinus" [^287]. Here again Christian worship is reduced to evening suppers, wherein, as in Justin, a prominent place is occupied at this sacred supper by the reading of Holy Scripture, which Pliny, apparently, does not mention. One may think that this element of divine service now came forward for the first time, occupying a distinct and prominent position alongside hymns and prayers, thanks unto the final formation of the New Testament canon.

Thus, however fragmentary and few the reports of the 2nd century concerning the church services of that era may be, these reports authorize the conclusion that there existed two divine services during the course of the day—one morning, the other evening. The first consisted of hymns and prayers and, perhaps, of the reading of Holy Scripture; at the second, in addition to all of this, the Eucharist and the agape were celebrated. The second service was the more essential, behind which the first remained, as it were, in the shadow, which is why a number of the enumerated monuments make no mention whatever of the first, morning service without the Eucharist and agape. Nor is it mentioned by the *Teaching of the Twelve Apostles*, which speaks of the Eucharist and the agape repeatedly [^288]. One cannot fail to observe during the course of the 2nd century a gradual process of the separation of the Eucharist from the agape as its independent and most sacred part, whereas in the apostolic century they, apparently, merged into one single sacred whole (moreover, in the Apostle Paul also one discerns a desire for such separation in his instructions unto the Corinthians concerning the reverent reception of the Eucharistic Gifts). Already the *Teaching of the Twelve Apostles* speaks of the Eucharist without mentioning agapes. In Justin, moreover, the agape is completely lost beside the Eucharist, being neither described nor named. In any case, the Eucharist at the agapes now began to precede the partaking of other food.
*(Orig. p. 56)*

### Days of Divine Worship

Remarkable also is the fact that the majority of monuments of the 2nd century speak of divine worship only on the day of the Lord / Sunday (besides Pliny and Justin, also the *Teaching of the Twelve Apostles*: **"On the Lord's Day, gathering together, break bread and give thanks, having first confessed your sins, that your sacrifice may be pure"**

---

=== LEAF p53 ===
[that your sacrifice may be pure] [^289]. Yet in view of the fact that both from the apostolic century and from the 3rd century we possess testimonies concerning the daily celebration of the Eucharist, we must think that such a practice existed now as well. Presumably on Sundays there were general assemblies of the Christians of an entire city, of the whole episcopate and eparchy, and on weekdays private assemblies in houses [^290]. Eparchies at that time were often no larger than present-day parishes [^291], so that general assemblies took place for the most part at the bishop's: **"Take heed to have but one Eucharist,"** writes St. Ignatius unto the Philadelphians, **"for there is one flesh of our Lord Jesus Christ, and one cup unto the unity of His blood, one altar, as there is one bishop with the presbytery and deacons; where the shepherd is, thither do ye follow as sheep"** [^292]. Still, the exhortation to have one Eucharist with the bishop shows that it was not always solitary for the Christians of a given city. In another place St. Ignatius exhorts not to hold prayer assemblies without the bishop, or at least without the presbyters [^293].

### Hours of Prayer

There was not abandoned in the 2nd century the custom, adopted by the first Christians from the Old Testament Church, of sanctifying with prayer three most important times of the day—morning, midday, and evening. **"Neither pray ye as the hypocrites"** (that is, the Jews, as the context shows), says the *Teaching of the Twelve Apostles*, **"but as the Lord commanded in His Gospel, after this manner pray ye: Our Father..."**—the Lord's Prayer is cited, and there is added: **"Thrice a day pray ye thus"** [^294]. Here one may see an indication of the continued use here and there among Christians even of this time of Jewish prayers (the *Shema* and *Shemoneh Esreh*) for the three hours of prayer, and a decisive demand to replace these prayers with a purely Christian one. As these hours of prayer, monuments of the apostolic era and the 3rd century designate the 3rd, 6th, and 9th. Consequently, they were honored with prayer in the 2nd century as well. Thus *(Orig. p. 57)* we have here the first germ of the present-day Order of the Hours: it consists as yet of the Lord's Prayer alone, which even now occupies in the Hours a central and primary place. The Hours do not yet possess a Christian symbolic meaning, but are sanctified by prayer simply as the most important divisions of the day.

### Order of Service

As regards the composition of the services of that time, the reports already cited outline it in general terms. A series of other reports allows us to say even more upon this question. First of all, it is beyond doubt that in divine worship there already existed a rather firmly and definitely established *ordo*, an order, departure from which

---

=== LEAF p54 ===
was forbidden. Clement, Bishop of Rome, writes unto the Corinthians (ca. 100): **"We ought to do all things in order, whatsoever the Master hath commanded us to perform at appointed seasons. He hath commanded the oblations and divine services (*prosphoras kai leitourgias*—presumably the Eucharist and other services) to be celebrated not carelessly nor without order, but at fixed times and hours. Where also and through whom He willeth them to be performed, He Himself hath determined by His own supreme will. They therefore that make their offerings at the appointed seasons are acceptable and blessed"** [^295].

### Constituent Parts of Divine Worship

#### Prayer

The first and most important constituent part of every divine service, *prayer*, is regarded by St. Ignatius as the best instrument of church communion, upon condition of close union therein: **"In the common assembly let there be one prayer, one supplication, one mind, one hope."** Thus, unto prayer and union therein there was now principally reduced that *koinōnia* (communion) which in the apostolic era was a constant co-presence or common life of the faithful, entirely possible given their small numbers at that time [^296]. The principal subject of prayers was brethren of the same faith: **"Pray for all the saints,"** exhorts St. Polycarp [^297]; yet according to the commandment of the Apostle, prayer extended also unto all men: **"And pray ye without ceasing for other men also; for there is in them a hope of repentance, that they may attain unto God,"** writes St. Ignatius [^298]. **"Pray also for kings, and for powers, and for princes, even for them that persecute you, and for enemies of the Cross,"** exhorts St. Polycarp [^299]. As we have seen, the *Teaching of the Twelve Apostles* furnishes, presumably, the most ancient written formula of Eucharistic prayer; it also points unto the use of the Lord's Prayer. Unto the existence of a series of definite prayers and unto the central position among them of the Lord's Prayer, apparently, points the expression of St. Polycarp: **"Let us be watchful in prayer and perse*(Orig. p. 58)*vere in fasting; in supplications beseeching the all-seeing God not to lead us into temptation"** [^300].

#### Psalmody and Hymns

Further than prayer advanced the development of another constituent part of divine worship—*chanting / hymnody*. We have seen how Pliny reduces to it the entire morning service. In St. Ignatius's farewell epistles church hymns sound forth, as it were, unceasingly, revealing in him a great lover of singing: **"Form a choir in love and sing praises unto the Father in Christ Jesus"** [^301]; **"Join into a choir, every one of you, that being harmonious in love and taking up the song of God in unison, ye may sing with one voice unto the Father through Jesus Christ"** [^302]. Therefore

---

=== LEAF p55 ===
one cannot suspect of historical unreliability the report of Socrates' *Ecclesiastical History* that it was precisely Ignatius who introduced antiphonal chanting into the Church of Antioch [^303]. If so, then the antiphonal manner of singing became thus already from the 2nd century predominant and characteristic in the Eastern Church (the three-antiphon system) in contrast unto the Western, where hypophonic and epiphonic chanting (responsories) was more accepted. What a profound impression was produced at first by the antiphonal manner of singing, alien unto primitive antiquity (in Old Testament worship, as we have seen, its existence is doubtful), is shown by the tradition which the historian Socrates appends unto his report concerning the introduction of antiphonal chanting by St. Ignatius—namely, that the thought of this singing was inspired in Ignatius by a vision of angels who thus precisely glorified God. And how new antiphonal singing was for the Christian Church of that era is shown by the slowness and difficulty with which it spread. In that very Antioch in which the origin of this chanting is linked with the name of Ignatius, there labored in the 4th century upon its establishment, according to the testimony of Bl. Theodoret, the presbyters Diodore and Flavian [^304]. When St. Basil the Great introduced this singing into the Church of Neocaesarea, its clergy resisted, appealing unto the ancient practice of congregational singing received from the founder of the Church, St. Gregory of Neocaesarea, unto which Basil the Great replies unto the Neocaesareans that antiphonal singing had already been accepted in Egypt, Libya, Palestine, Arabia, Phoenicia, and Syria. In the West this singing was first introduced by St. Ambrose (4th cent.) *(Orig. p. 59)* into the Church of Milan [^305], whence it was transferred unto North Africa by St. Augustine (5th cent.), and in the Roman Church it was adopted under Pope Celestine I (422–432) [^306].

The innovation of St. Ignatius by its mere fact demonstrates how greatly the chant material of divine worship had increased by that time. The cause of this growth was presumably the hymnographic activity of the Gnostics, which impelled pastors to fight against them with the same weapon, that is, by composing their own hymns. Thus Valentinus and other Gnostics composed psalms for the propagation of their false teaching [^307]. The hymns of Bardesanes survived down to the time of St. Ephrem the Syrian (4th cent.) [^308]. Yet of Orthodox hymnographers of the 2nd century there has reached us only the name of the Martyr Athenogenes († 159), of whom St. Basil the Great says that before his death he composed a hymn that became exceedingly famous and was long preserved (this hymn, as we shall see, is considered to be **"O Gladsome Light"** [*Phōs Hilaron* / *Svete Tikhiy*]). Unto St. Justin Martyr is attributed [^309] the composition of a lost book, *Psaltēs* (The Singer / Psalmist), which presumably constituted a collection of songs (termed psalms, perhaps because paganism had made the designation "hymns" odious unto Christians) [^310]. Eusebius cites a 3rd-century treatise against the heresy of Artemon, wherein it is stated among other things: **"How many psalms and songs (*ōdai*) were written from the beginning by faithful brethren, praising (*hymnousi*"**

---

=== LEAF p56 ===
[*theologountes*] **Christ as the Word of God"** [^311]. In the martyrdom acts of St. Ignatius it is related that the venerators of the saint chanted hymns at his tomb for several days [^312].

### Reading of Holy Scripture: Formation of the New Testament Canon

The initial history of the *liturgical use of Holy Scripture* is closely bound up with the history of the New Testament canon. The first definite testimony concerning the reading of Holy Scripture during Christian divine worship is the above-cited passage from St. Justin Martyr concerning the reading in the "assembly of the brethren" of the "memoirs of the Apostles and the writings of the Prophets," a passage speaking of the practice ca. 140. That by this time the New Testament scriptures, and that in almost the entire scope of their present canon, had received in *(Orig. p. 60)* the eyes of Christians an authority equal unto the Old Testament is proved by the constant references unto them in contemporary works and by the striving to establish harmony between the two testaments (with this latter, one might say almost exclusively, the *Epistle of Barnabas*, appearing at the end of the 1st century, is already occupied). Of course, such reverence toward themselves and the honorary place in divine worship connected with this reverence the New Testament writings acquired not at once and not without a struggle. In this regard great importance must have belonged unto the idea of the New Testament in general expressed by the Apostle Paul and developed by him in detail, which assigned unto the entire Old Testament, and consequently unto its scriptures, an apparently not very elevated place. Carried unto an extreme by Marcion, this idea led in the latter's case even unto the complete rejection of the Old Testament and the striving to replace all its books with the Pauline epistles. Marcion's teaching upon this point was undoubtedly a continuation of those disputes regarding both testaments which began already under the Apostles between Jewish Christians and Gentile Christians, and an echo of which even in the 4th century is the recognition of the Catholic Epistles and the Apocalypse, belonging unto Apostles of Hebrew stock, as "disputed" (*antilegomena*). Only gradually could Jewish Christians be inclined toward recognizing the New Testament in that corpus wherein there entered into it not only the writings of the Apostles of the circumcision, but also of authors who declared themselves apostles of the Gentiles, that is, Paul and his assistants (among the latter could be numbered also the Evangelist Mark, who stood as a mediator, as it were, between both tendencies, and was therefore placed between Matthew and Luke [^313]). On the other hand, Gentile Christians also, only with the final clarification in the ecclesiastical consciousness of the degree of obligatoriness which the Old Testament law could still possess, were able to call the Old Testament their own and set the Apostle Peter on a par with the Apostle Paul. As the heresies of the Ebionites, and especially of the Gnostics and Manichaeans demonstrate, the 2nd century is still a witness of this struggle between two tendencies in Christianity, and it serves as the greatest honor for the Church of Christ that,

---

=== LEAF p57 ===
having initially been formed in an overwhelming majority from Gentiles, and in the 2nd century having broken completely with Judaism, she resisted on the question of the inspired canon and its use in divine worship a current so natural under these circumstances as Marcionism, and declared not only the writings of all the Apostles to be of equal value, but also the Old Testament to be equal in divine inspiration unto the New. Yet the fact that according to St. Justin the reading of the "memoirs of the Apostles" preceded the "writings of the Prophets" clearly shows at what stage in the 2nd century the question concerning the significance of the Old Testament still stood: in the 3rd century Old Testament readings already precede New Testament ones. Traces of this struggle between the two tendencies of theological thought—still so fierce in the 2nd century—concerning the comparative significance of the writings of the Apostles of the circumcision and the Apostle Paul have been preserved also by our present order of liturgical readings from the New Testament: the Catholic Epistles are not read at all on Sun*(Orig. p. 61)*days, and in the church year they follow after the Pauline epistles; the Gospel of Mark is read in Great Lent and occupies in the annual cycle of readings the same place as the Catholic Epistles; an exception is made for the Evangelist Matthew, as the most ancient and most revered in the primitive Church.

### The Gospel and the Apostle

In the New Testament canon already in the 2nd century there are clearly segregated as two parts thereof—the Gospels, on the one hand, and the other apostolic writings, on the other. **"Let us have recourse unto the Gospel as unto the flesh of Jesus,"** says St. Ignatius already, **"and unto the Apostles as unto the presbytery of the Church"** [^314]. St. Irenaeus already proves the impossibility of any other number of Gospels than four:

> "For since there are four zones of the world in which we live, and four principal winds, and since the Church is scattered throughout all the earth, and the pillar and ground of the Church is the Gospel and the Spirit of life, it is fitting that she should have four pillars, breathing out incorruption from every side, and vivifying men. And the Cherubim, too, have four faces, and their countenances are images of the dispensation of the Son of God... The living creatures are quadriform, and quadriform is the Gospel and the working of the Lord. And for this reason four principal covenants were given unto the human race: one under Adam before the Flood, another after the Flood under Noah, the third the giving of the Law under Moses, and the fourth that which reneweth man and recapitulateth all things in itself through the Gospel... Seeing that God made all things harmonious and well-ordered, it was fitting that the Gospel also should possess an aspect well-arranged and harmonious. Having examined the opinion of those who handed down the Gospel unto us upon the basis of their own principles, let us now pass on unto

---

=== LEAF p58 ===
the remaining Apostles" [^315].

Yet St. Irenaeus lived at the close of the 2nd century. St. Justin's expression concerning the liturgical reading of the New Testament does not yet draw so sharp a line between the Gospels and the remaining apostolic writings. Characteristic in this expression is the designation of the apostolic writings as *memoirs*, when the majority of them bear the superscription of epistles and possess the character of the latter, and when it would have been most exact to designate their totality as *writings*, as the Old Testament books are called in St. Justin. Does not such a designation point unto the preferential use during the divine service of that time of apostolic writings of a narrative character, that is, the Gospels and Acts? In our present divine worship also the Gospel possesses a broader use than the Apostle; while in Coptic worship, which has preserved traces of a profoundly ancient practice, a Gospel is appointed at every service of the daily cycle. At the same time, Justin's expression may indicate that the Gospels in his time were not sharply segregated from Acts, which was so natural given the common authorship of the latter with the Third Gospel. Even in 3rd-century monuments, as we shall see, the New Testament is divided into three parts: Gospel, Acts, and Epistles, and not into two (Gospel and Apostle). *(Orig. p. 62)* A trace of such an exalted ancient view of the Acts in our present divine worship is their use during the Pentecostarion period [Paschal season]. Unto granting the Gospels an honorary place in the New Testament canon there could easily and early contribute their analogy with the 'Law' (Torah) of the Old Testament canon, which in antiquity was divided into two main parts—the Law and the Prophets. It seems that the Gospels themselves also have in view their analogy with the Pentateuch, when, for example, the Gospel of Matthew titles its first section **"The book of the generation"** (*biblos geneseōs*, Slav. *kniga rodstva*), while the Gospel of John begins with the opening words of the Book of Genesis [^316].

### The Manner of Reading Holy Scripture

As regards the *manner of reading* Holy Scripture in divine worship in the 2nd century, the sole above-cited 2nd-century testimony concerning this reading (that of St. Justin) allows us to say that it was performed by a reader (*ho anagignōskōn*), and not by the president of the assembly (bishop or presbyter), who came forward after the reading with the homily. An echo of such a division of reading and preaching in divine worship in our present services is the obligatory reading of the Gospel at the Liturgy by the deacon, and not by the priest. Concerning the selection of readings nothing is known.

---

=== LEAF p59 ===
### Preaching / The Homily

With the reading of Holy Scripture *preaching* stood in intimate connection. How essential a place it occupied at every liturgical assembly is demonstrated by the exhortation of the Apostle Barnabas: **"Ye ought not to separate yourselves from others, but coming together into one place, inquire what is fitting and profitable for all the beloved in common"** [^317]. Initially in the 2nd century, given the incomplete universal familiarity with the apostolic writings, the vacillations regarding their authenticity provoked by the appearance of apocrypha, and the ripening opposition unto Judaism which was unfavorable unto the widespread use of Old Testament scriptures in divine worship, preaching during divine service was able, if not to replace the reading of Scripture—resting constantly and wholly upon it—at least to occupy a more prominent position therein. Unto this there also contributed the continuing operation in the Church of spiritual gifts—apostleship, prophecy, and teaching—which imparted unto ecclesiastical preaching almost the same stamp of inspiration as that possessed by the writings of the Apostles themselves. *(Orig. p. 63)* At least the writings of such apostles, prophets, and teachers as Barnabas and Hermas were long set on a par with apostolic writings. Yet Hermas also sends out and publishes his prophecies through the mediation of presbyters headed by Clement, remarking: "for unto him (Clement) this is committed," just as he reads them in the city together with the "presbyters, presidents of the Church" [^318]. Thus the right of preaching is increasingly confirmed as belonging unto the ecclesiastical hierarchy. In Justin's testimony, consequently around the middle of the 2nd century, in the Church only the president preaches, although spiritual prophetic gifts exist even in the time of Irenaeus [^319]. The further on, however, the more preaching in divine worship was bound to yield its former place unto the reading of Holy Scripture. Our present Typikon, in appointing for the morning service the reading of patristic homilies, seeks to preserve the memory of the most ancient type of divine worship.

### The Church Year in the II Century: Sunday

The church year in the 2nd century develops just as much through the multiplication of feasts and commemorations as through the strengthening and deepening of those inherited from the preceding period. Thus Sunday completely supplants the Sabbath. It alone is mentioned as a weekly festal day in the Apostle Barnabas, in Pliny, Justin, and in the *Teaching of the Twelve Apostles*. Of the Sabbath not only does no monument make mention, but its abrogation for the New Testament is proved alongside circumcision and sacrifices, as an institution called forth by the hardness of heart of the Jews [^320]. **"Behold how He saith,"** writes Barnabas: **"Your present sabbaths are not acceptable unto Me, but those which I have appointed, which shall dawn"**

---

=== LEAF p60 ===
**"when, having set an end unto all things, I shall make the beginning of the eighth day, that is, the beginning of another world." Wherefore also we keep the eighth day with joyfulness, on which also Jesus rose again from the dead** [^321]. Here is ascertained the complete replacement of the Jewish Sabbath by Sunday, wherein this replacement is justified from the Old Testament itself, and unto Sunday there is attributed, besides the commemoration of Christ's resurrection, a symbolic meaning analogous with the Sabbath. (This day is called the eighth as following upon the seventh, the Sabbath; cf. its symbolic meaning in Justin [^322]). In St. Ignatius the God-Bearer Sunday is called *Kyriakē* [^323]—a term representing an abbreviation of the apocalyptic *Kyriakē hēmera* [the Lord's Day].

### Pascha

In this same century there is undoubtedly already honored in a special manner the annual day of the death and resurrection of Christ, that is, the feast of *Pascha* (its existence in the apostolic century, as we have seen, is only probable).
*(Orig. p. 64)*
A fragment of the utmost value in this regard from the letter of St. Irenaeus, Bishop of Lyons, unto Victor, Bishop of Rome, preserved by Eusebius, sheds light upon the primitive character of the feast of Pascha. The epistle was written concerning the disputes over the time of the celebration of Pascha, which began already under St. Polycarp, Bishop of Smyrna († 167), provoked a series of councils, and continued with still greater intensity, apparently, under St. Irenaeus, Bishop of Lyons († 202). The disputes concerned whether Pascha should be celebrated together with the Jewish Passover on the 14th–15th day of the first spring lunar month, or on the first Sunday after that day.

### St. Irenaeus on Pascha

One need only read attentively the aforementioned fragment, paying heed unto every word thereof, in order to make it clear that the dispute itself concerning the time of Pascha arose because by this time the character of the feast itself, the view of it, was beginning gradually to change; namely, whereas previously Pascha was viewed as a fast in honor of the death of the Savior, Who died precisely on the day of the Jewish Passover, now they wished to unite with it also the joyful commemoration of Christ's resurrection, which did not accord with fasting and was more fitting not for any single day of the week upon which the Jewish Passover might fall, but for Sunday. In Rome Pascha had already begun to acquire a second keynote, so to speak; while in Asia Minor, where life did not move with such rapidity, there was preserved, presumably, the original, most ancient view of Pascha. Therefore in the dispute the bishops simply failed to understand one another. Still less could
"""

